== Chapter I

#align(center)[#block(width: 85%)[
  #set par(justify: false)
  #strong[The Proem, or Introduction.]
]]

#v(0.9em)

#align(center)[
  #block(width: 85%, breakable: false)[
    #set par(justify: false)
    #text(style: "italic")[#emph[Rom 8:28.] We know that all things work together for good to those who love God, to those who are called according to His purpose.]
  ]
]

#v(0.8em)

#smallcaps[If] the whole Scripture is the feast of the soul (as St. #emph[Ambrose] says), then this chapter may be a dish at this feast, which with its sweet variety may greatly refresh and enliven the hearts of God’s people. In the verses before, the apostle had been wading through the great doctrines of justification and adoption, mysteries so hard and so deep that without the help and guidance of the Spirit he might soon have waded out of his depth. In this verse the apostle touches that pleasant string of consolation: “We know that all things work together for good to those who love God.” Every word is weighty, so I will gather up every filing of this gold, that nothing may be lost.

In the text there are three general branches. 1. A glorious privilege: #emph[All things work for good.] 2. The persons who have a share in this privilege, and they are described in two ways: 1. they are #emph[lovers of God]\; 2. they are #emph[called.] 3. The origin and spring of this effectual calling, set down in these words, κατὰ πρόθεσιν, #emph[according to His purpose.] I will treat all these in order, and I begin with the first.

1\. The glorious privilege, in which there are two things to consider: 1. The certainty of the privilege, ὄιδαμὲν, #emph[We know.] 2. The excellence of the privilege, παντα συνεργει εἰς τὸ ἀγαθὸν, #emph[All things work together for good.]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[1\. The #emph[certainty] of the privilege: #emph[We know.] It is not a matter hanging in doubt. The apostle does not say, We hope, or, We guess; it is like an article in our creed: #emph[We know all things work for good.]]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[From this observe #emph[that the truths of the gospel] #footnote[#emph[Doctrine.]] #emph[are evident and infallible.]]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[A Christian may come not only to a loose opinion but to a certainty about what he holds. As the axioms in logic and the aphorisms in medicine are demonstrated to reason, so the truths in religion are demonstrated to faith. #emph[We know,] says the apostle. Though a Christian does not have a perfect knowledge of the mysteries of the gospel, yet he has a certain knowledge. “We see but a dim reflection as in a mirror” #footnote[ἐν αἰνίγματι.] (1 Cor 13:12); so we do not have perfection of knowledge. But we behold “with unveiled faces” #footnote[προσώπῳ.] (2 Cor 3:18); so we have a certainty. The Spirit of God imprints heavenly truths on the heart as with the point of a diamond. A Christian may know infallibly that there is an evil in sin and a beauty in holiness. He may know that he is in the state of grace: “We know that we have passed from death to life” (1 John 3:14). He may know that he will go to heaven: “We know that if the earthly tent we live in is dismantled, we have a building from God, an eternal house in heaven, not built by human hands” (2 Cor 5:1). The Lord does not leave his people in uncertainty in matters of salvation. The apostle comes in the text with his #emph[Probatum est] \[It is proved\]: #emph[We know.] We have arrived at a πεποίθησις, or holy confidence; we have both the Spirit of God and our own experience setting a seal to it.]]

#pad(left: 2.0em)[#par(first-line-indent: (amount: 12pt, all: true))[Let us not rest in skepticism,#footnote[#emph[Use.]] but labor to come to a certainty in the things of religion. As that martyr woman said, “I cannot dispute for Christ, but I can burn for Christ.” God knows whether we may not be called out to be witnesses to his truth, so it concerns us to be well grounded and confirmed in it. If we are doubtful Christians, we will be wavering Christians. Where does apostasy come from, but from unbelief? Men first question the truth, and then fall from the truth. O beg the Spirit of God not only to #emph[anoint] you#super[+], but to #emph[seal] you#super[+] (2 Cor 1:22).]]
